        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "I now have the full picture. Let me synthesize.\n\nThe question: \"What might someone pay for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of acompose or umbra? Give me price bands for each, and say whether anyone would actually buy one.\"\n\nKey facts:\n1. **acompose** = agent-compose. Renders a bundle (YAML roster from housecast) into harness surfaces without executable authority. \"the right specialist instantly, on any harness.\" One command materializes a named specialist. Sells to teams running agents against their own systems.\n2. **umbra** = the occlusion engine, renamed from cli-guard. argv validated before execve, scope token per verb, egress proxy, audit log, exit-code taxonomy. The wall is one file. Sells to teams running agents against their own systems. Ships no model (NDAA-clean).\n3. Rate is $150/hr, 70% utilization, confirmed. This is Kai's rule rate, not a floor. No walk-away/premium/negotiation ladder confirmed.\n4. \"Contracting, sponsorship, and SaaS stay hypotheses unless evidence establishes them.\" Portfolio director doctrine: never manufacture customers or revenue.\n5. business-disposition: do not promote a possible path into an established company, customer base, or offering without evidence.\n6. contracting-offers: \"All of them are one offer... separate offers make a buyer choose, which is friction.\" And \"What takes an offer off the table\" — umbra and acompose ship no model, NDAA-clean.\n7. No flagship, no customer base established.\n\nThe compensation skill has a hard rule: do not invent comp preferences. The rule rate is $150/hr. So the pricing bands must derive from the confirmed $150/hr rate, not from invented figures. I can compute bands from $150/hr × hours, but I must be careful: \"A rate and a salary floor are different units and do not compare.\" But here we're doing rate × hours for project pricing, which is legitimate — the question is about a custom one-time cut + long-term support, which is a services/contracting question.\n\nActually wait — the guardrail says every numeric estimate must go to a decision model (Jev). But this is a pricing analysis for Kai's own business, and the rate is already confirmed at $150/hr. The bands are arithmetic derivable from $150/hr × estimated hours. The guardrail: \"arithmetic that is deterministic stays local.\" So $150 × hours is deterministic arithmetic. The estimate of hours is a judgment, but I can present bands as \"at the confirmed $150/hr rule rate, a one-time cut priced at N hours is $X.\"\n\nBut the \"whether anyone would actually buy one\" is a go/no-go / likelihood question → that goes to Jev as a typed decision. Actually the guardrail says: \"Every probability, likelihood, numeric estimate (a price, a count, a duration), ranking, and go or no-go goes to the deployment's decision model as a typed question.\"\n\nSo the price bands themselves are partly a numeric estimate (the hours/duration). The \"would anyone buy\" is a go/no-go / likelihood. I should route these through Jev.\n\nI have the kai-server MCP tools (create_noul_decision, create_choice_decision, create_score_decision). Wait, but there's also the tailnet_coilyco_jev MCP listed in skills. But in my actual available tools, I have `kai-server_30089_mcp__create_noul_decision`, `create_choice_decision`, `create_score_decision`. Let me check what I have available.\n\nAvailable tools include:\n- kai-server_30089_mcp__create_choice_decision\n- kai-server_30089_mcp__create_noul_decision\n- kai-server_30089_mcp__create_score_decision\n- kai-server_30089_mcp__mcp_beaver_info\n\nSo Jev is available via these. The state is required and x-opcore-raw. Let me think about whether I actually need to call Jev.\n\nThe guardrail procedure: case against first, reversal condition, then decision-model call, then verdict.\n\nFor this question, the \"verdict\" would be about the price bands and whether anyone would buy. The price bands are arithmetic from the confirmed $150/hr rate, which is deterministic (arithmetic stays local). But the *hours estimate* for a \"custom one-time cut\" is a numeric estimate that the guardrail says should go to the decision model.\n\nHmm, but actually I need to think about what's genuinely decidable here vs. what's arithmetic.\n\nLet me think about the honest content:\n\n1. **Rate is confirmed**: $150/hr at 70% utilization. This is the anchor for any price band. I must not invent a different rate, premium tier, or ladder.\n\n2. **Price bands**: A \"custom one-time cut\" = scoping + build + integration for a specific client. Long-term support = retainer / ongoing maintenance.\n\nFor umbra (the occlusion engine / policy wall):\n- It's already built (shipped the `umbra` CLI, renamed from cli-guard). A \"custom one-time cut\" would be adapting it to a client's environment: wiring their verbs, their scope tokens, their egress proxy, their audit log destination. This is integration/config work, not greenfield.\n- Hours: maybe 20-60 hours for a one-time cut (scoping, guardfile authoring, deployment to their runtime). At $150/hr → $3,000–$9,000. \n- Long-term support: a retainer. At 70% utilization, a monthly retainer of say 10 hours = $1,500/mo, or 20 hours = $3,000/mo.\n\nFor acompose (agent-compose, renders bundle into harness surfaces):\n- This is more of a framework/CLI. A \"custom one-time cut\" = authoring a custom roster/bundle for a client's team + wiring their harness. It's more consulting-flavored: you materialize their specialists. Hours similar: 20-60 hours → $3,000–$9,000. But acompose is arguably less \"custom cut\" territory because it's the downstream renderer; the value is in the roster (housecast) + the render. Actually the contracting-offers says agent-compose's cell is \"the right specialist instantly on any harness.\"\n\nBut wait — I need to be careful. The question asks specifically about acompose and umbra. Both are in the four headlines. umbra = the wall (policy), acompose = the rendered bundle.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what \"custom one-time cut\" means. It likely means: take the OSS/portfolio project and do a custom build/deployment for a specific buyer — a one-time engagement to set it up for them, plus ongoing support.\n\n3. **Would anyone actually buy one?** This is the key question. The evidence:\n- umbra's buyer: teams running agents against their own systems, worried about what the agent can touch. This is a real, growing market (agent safety/guardrails). Kong, Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, Permit.io, thirteen named gateways are all in the MCP tool ACL space (from saas-wedges). BUT — the saas-wedges entry says mcp-beaver's generation half is \"commoditized\" by those thirteen gateways, and wedge 4 was closed. However umbra is different: umbra is the argv-level occlusion engine (before execve), not the MCP tool ACL layer. umbra ships no denylist, one file.\n- acompose's buyer: teams running multiple harnesses (Claude Code, Codex, Goose, OpenCode) who want consistent specialist roles. This is a narrower, more self-serve-y tool.\n\nThe honest read from the doctrine: **no established customer base exists; contracting is a hypothesis**. \"Do not promote a possible path into an established company, customer base, or offering without evidence.\" So \"would anyone buy\" cannot be answered \"yes\" from evidence — it's a hypothesis. I should say: the buyer exists in the market (there's real demand for agent guardrails), but no confirmed buyer exists for Kai's specific offers, and there's no signal of anyone having asked.\n\nAlso the credential-legibility reference (not loaded) mentions \"the technical-buyer audience mismatch\" and \"practitioner surfaces miss buyers\" — suggesting the practical answer is \"no, not yet, because the demand signal and buyer reach aren't there.\"\n\nLet me consider whether to load credential-legibility.md, since it's directly relevant to \"would anyone actually buy.\" The contracting-offers.md related section says: \"lore-self-credential-legibility for why practitioner surfaces miss buyers.\" That's directly on point for \"would anyone buy.\" Let me load it.\n\nAlso there's the outward personality requiring me to WebSearch before making market claims (novelty, scarcity, popularity). The question asks about what someone might pay and whether anyone would buy — these are market claims. The boundary-seek-external-validation says I own the reach outside the local frame. So I should WebSearch to ground the \"would anyone buy\" and \"what would they pay\" claims in the actual agent-guardrails market.\n\nLet me plan:\n1. Load credential-legibility.md (relevant to buyer mismatch).\n2. WebSearch for market references: what do agent guardrail/security tools cost, retainer rates for devtools consulting, etc. Specifically:\n   - \"MCP tool ACL / agent guardrail\" pricing (Kong AI Gateway pricing, etc.)\n   - devtools founder/consulting rates, custom deployment engagements.\n   - whether there's demand for agent security tooling.\n\nActually, let me be efficient. The core answer doesn't need exhaustive research. The outward personality + boundary requires at least a real external check before comparative/novelty/market claims. I'll do a couple targeted web searches to ground: (a) what comparable agent-guardrail/security tools charge, (b) whether there's an actual market for \"custom deployment + support\" of devtools.\n\nLet me do the credential-legibility load + web searches in parallel. Actually load_skill and web-search (shell?) — wait, I have `web-search`? Let me check my tools. I don't see a `web_search` tool in my function list. Wait, the skills mention `web-search` as a skill. Let me check — the \"Skills\" section lists `web-search` as a skill: \"Search the web and extract page content using DuckDuckGo...\". So there's a web-search skill, not a tool. Hmm.\n\nActually looking at my available function tools, I don't have a native web search tool. I have `shell` which could be used with curl, but that's hacky. Let me check — the skills list has \"web-search\" as a skill. So I should load the web-search skill to get web search capability.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider. Do I have web search? The tools available are: analyze, apps_*, delegate, edit, extensionmanager_*, kai-server MCP (jev), load, load_skill, read_image, shell, todo, tree, write. No web_search function directly.\n\nBut the \"Skills\" section lists `web-search` as a skill. Skills are loaded via load_skill. Let me load web-search to see what it provides.\n\nActually, the boundary-seek-external-validation doctrine and the outward personality both require external validation. The role's \"Run\" section explicitly says: \"WebSearch the claim before ranking it... report how many independent results came back.\" So web search is expected. Let me load the web-search skill.\n\nLet me do this: load web-search skill + load credential-legibility.md in parallel.",
