thored) + per-harness render config. It's more of a consulting engagement. But acompose itself is thin — \"renders the bundle into harness surfaces without executable authority.\" The custom work is smaller in mechanism but the value is in specialist-authoring. Realistic 20–50 hours. At $150 → **$3,000–$7,500**. Band: **$3k–$8k**.\n\nActually, I should be careful about the direction. Let me reconsider what a \"custom one-time cut\" means for each, distinct from the product itself.\n\nThe question is literally: \"What might someone pay for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of acompose or umbra?\"\n\nSo: the buyer licenses/customizes the OSS + gets a one-time customization (\"cut\") + long-term support. This is a productized-service/contracting framing.\n\numbra: higher ceiling (the wall is the most defensible, NDAA-clean, sells to teams running agents against their own systems — the largest and most urgent buyer). Custom cut = integration into their runtime.\nacompose: narrower (harness portability specialist roles). Custom cut = roster + harness wiring.\n\nNow the \"would anyone actually buy one\" — this is the crux. The guardrail says go/no-go + numeric estimates go to Jev.\n\nLet me construct a Jev call. I have create_noul_decision (yes/no) and create_score_decision (ordered levels) and create_choice_decision.\n\nFor \"would anyone actually buy,\" I could use a noul (yes/no) question. But actually the honest answer needs nuance: \"buy\" at what price band, which product. Let me think about what's genuinely decidable.\n\nThe guardrail wants: case against first (in my own words), reversal condition, then a separate decision model gives the verdict, then I report it.\n\nLet me structure the actual output:\n1. The call first (decisive): my recommendation.\n2. Case against (strongest).\n3. Reversal condition.\n4. Jev verdict (the number/likelihood from the decision model).\n5. Price bands.\n6. \"Would anyone buy\" answer.\n\nI need to actually call Jev. Let me figure out the right shape.\n\nFor the price bands: the guardrail says numeric estimates (a price, a count, a duration) go to the decision model. But the rate is confirmed ($150/hr), and hours × rate is deterministic arithmetic. The genuinely uncertain numeric is the *hours* (duration) and the *probability a buyer exists*.\n\nI think the cleanest approach:\n- Use Jev `score` for the duration/size estimate? Or use Jev `noul` for \"would anyone buy.\"\n- The hours estimate: I can present bands as arithmetic from $150/hr with my own scoping, clearly labeled as my estimate, then have Jev adjudicate the \"would anyone buy\" go/no-go and maybe the realistic band.\n\nActually, re-reading the guardrail: \"Every probability, likelihood, numeric estimate (a price, a count, a duration), ranking, and go or no-go goes to the deployment's decision model as a typed question.\" And \"arithmetic that is deterministic stays local.\"\n\nSo: the price bands = $150/hr × hours. The $150/hr is confirmed (local, deterministic). The hours is a numeric estimate → should go to Jev. Hmm, but Jev answers \"typed shapes\" (yes/no, choice, score) — it can't directly produce a dollar figure or an hour count. It answers a score (ordered level) or a choice or yes/no.\n\nSo I can frame the hours as a `score` question with ordered levels, or a `choice` with band options. For the go/no-go (would anyone buy), use `noul`.\n\nLet me design the Jev questions:\n\n**Question 1 (noul): \"Would anyone actually buy\"** — for umbra specifically (the more defensible one).\nActually, let me do two nouls or one noul + one score.\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest and actually useful:\n\nJev call 1 — `noul`: \"Is there a real buyer who would pay for a custom one-time cut + support of umbra?\" with state containing the evidence both ways. Options: true/false criteria.\n\nJev call 2 — `noul` or `choice`: for acompose.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The guardrail says the verdict must be sourced outside this sea