ict), OR route the hours/size through Jev as a `score` question.\n\nLet me route the \"would anyone buy\" through Jev (noul), and also route a `choice` on which product is more likely to sell (since that's a ranking, and rankings go to the decision model). Actually \"say whether anyone would actually buy one\" — the comparative \"which one\" is useful.\n\nLet me settle on two Jev calls:\n1. `noul`: Would anyone pay for a custom one-time cut + long-term support of umbra (within a reasonable window)?\n2. `noul`: Would anyone pay for a custom one-time cut + long-term support of acompose?\n\nAnd maybe a third `choice` for which of the two is the stronger buy — but that's a ranking I can derive from the nouls. Actually the guardrail says ranking goes to decision model. But I can keep it to the two nouls and a choice.\n\nHmm, let me also consider: the price band itself. The guardrail lists \"a price\" as something that goes to the decision model. But Jev's tools can't emit a price; they emit typed decisions. The best I can do is have Jev score the *size* of the engagement (e.g., \"the realistic one-time cut size for umbra is: small/mid/large\") via `score`, then I map that to hours × $150.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about what actually serves the user well while honoring the guardrail.\n\nThe user wants: price bands for each, and a yes/no on whether anyone would buy.\n\nMy plan:\n1. Report the confirmed anchor ($150/hr) and note the anti-invention rule — I cannot invent a different rate.\n2. Give price bands as $150/hr × scoped hours, with hours labeled as my estimate.\n3. Route \"would anyone buy\" through Jev (noul), state evidence both ways neutrally.\n4. Report Jev's verdict with its confidence.\n5. Give my case-against-first and reversal condition.\n\nActually — the guardrail's order is strict: case against, reversal, decision-model call, verdict. So I should present case-against first, then reversal, then do the Jev call, then report verdict.\n\nLet me now actually make the Jev calls. I need to design the state object neutrally.\n\nState (shared for both questions) — describe the situation with evidence both ways, no conclusion:\n\n```\n{\n  \"project\": \"umbra\",\n  \"what_it_is\": \"CLI occlusion engine: validates argv before execve, per-verb scope tokens, per-invocation egress proxy, appended audit log, published exit-code taxonomy. Policy is one file, ships no denylist.\",\n  \"offer_shape\": \"custom one-time integration cut into a buyer's runtime + long-term support retainer\",\n  \"rate\": \"$150/hr confirmed rule rate at 70% utilization\",\n  \"evidence_for_demand\": [\n    \"agent-guardrail market is real and growing; Kong AI Gateway 3.13 shipped MCP tool ACLs (default-deny) and Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, Permit.io are all in the tool-ACL space\",\n    \"guardrail SaaS products exist at low price points (AgentGuard Pro $39/mo, Team $79/mo)\",\n    \"no model shipped, so NDAA Section 1532 does not block defense/intelligence-adjacent buyers\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_against_demand\": [\n    \"no established customer base; contracting is still a hypothesis in the portfolio\",\n    \"Kai's current surfaces (code freeze, livestreams, talks) reach practitioners/workers, not technical buyers\",\n    \"the MCP-tool-ACL layer is already commoditized by thirteen named gateways, which puts downward pressure on the guardrail layer umbra overlaps with\",\n    \"no confirmed inbound interest or ask\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nFor acompose:\n```\n{\n  \"project\": \"acompose (agent-compose)\",\n  \"what_it_is\": \"renders a YAML roster/bundle into harness surfaces (Claude Code, Codex, Goose, OpenCode) without executable authority; one command materializes a named specialist with voice, boundaries, tool inventory\",\n  \"offer_shape\": \"custom one-time cut (author a client's roster + wire their harnesses) + long-term support\",\n  \"rate\": \"$150/hr\",\n  \"evidence_for_demand\": [\"multi-harness teams exist who 