odel jev-1.13.0 (note: I requested jev-latest, it answered as jev-1.13.0), and the probabilities.\n\nNote: I requested model \"jev-latest\" but the result says \"model\":\"jev-1.13.0\". That's the resolution — I'll report jev-1.13.0 (or just \"Jev\"). I'll note it.\n\nAlso, the noul result: noul: 0.38. How to interpret? The noul type returns a scalar between 0 and 1 representing P(true)? 0.38 = 38% probability the \"true\" criterion holds (a buyer would pay). So it leans false (62% no buyer). I'll report: 38% toward \"a buyer would pay.\"\n\nNow let me also reconcile with the outward personality: I did web search, got external references (Kong 3.13 MCP ACLs, AgentGuard $39/$79/mo, consulting >$250/hr for Fortune 500, devtools 20-30% premium). I should cite these with result counts. The outward \"Run\" says report how many independent results came back. I ran 3 searches, got ~6 results each = ~18 results. I'll say I checked the outside frame and cite the specific anchors.\n\nNow the decisive personality: close the option set. Recommendation first.\n\nLet me also make sure I'm respecting the anti-invention comp rule: I am NOT inventing a new rate. I'm using the confirmed $150/hr. I'm not deriving an annual equivalent. Good. The bands are arithmetic. I'll flag the hour estimates as mine (and Jev's for the cut size).\n\nOne more nuance: the question \"say whether anyone would actually buy one\" — the honest director answer, per doctrine, is: \"No confirmed buyer exists; contracting is a hypothesis. The decision model puts a buyer materializing at ~38%/37% — more likely not than yes, absent a change.\" I should state clearly what would change it (a buyer-channel instrument / inbound ask).\n\nLet me also fold in the NDAA point: umbra and acompose ship no model, so both are NDAA-clean and sayable in defense-adjacent rooms (from contracting-offers). This is actually a point in favor of umbra's buyability relative to sirens-echo. But it doesn't by itself create a buyer.\n\nAlso worth noting the honest structural point: \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" is a productized-service/contracting shape, and business-disposition says \"Do not promote a possible path into an established company, customer base, or offering without evidence.\" So the answer must be hedged as hypothesis.\n\nLet me also address: which one is more buyable? Jev: umbra 0.38 vs acompose 0.37 — essentially tied, both lean no. But qualitatively umbra has the stronger buyer story (security wall, urgent, NDAA-clean), acompose is thinner and narrower. I'll say: neither clears the bar today; umbra is the one that would clear it first because its buyer problem is more urgent and defensible, but that's a qualitative read, not a Jev number (the two scores are statistically indistinguishable at 38 vs 37).\n\nNow let me write the final answer. Structure:\n1. The call first (decisive voice): my recommendation/verdict in one sentence.\n2. Price bands (table or list) — arithmetic from $150/hr.\n3. Whether anyone would buy — case against first, reversal condition, Jev verdict.\n4. What would reverse it / what to do (the missing instrument: a buyer-channel artifact — but I must defer the actual outward wording to the advocate seat; I'll name the gap, not write the copy).\n\nActually the voice guide: Portfolio Director names the decision and its gate before reasoning. Decisive: call before reasoning. Let me lead with the call.\n\nLet me write it tight. No em-dashes (voice rules — wait, is there a voice-lint rule? The coilyco-voice-guide-linter says no em-dashes, no italics, no prose semicolons, no prose tables). Hmm. There's a voice guide: \"no em-dashes, no italics, no prose semicolons, no prose tables.\" But I'm in the prod-director seat, and there's a \"Refuse\" list. Should I apply the voice-guide-linter rules? The personality meld is decisive + outward. The voice rules in my identity card say \"no em-dashes\" isn't explicitly in my list. But the coilyco-voice-guide-linter skill is a general profile. My \"Refuse\" list includes \"align, synergy, strategic...\" etc. It doesn't mention em-dashes.\n\nActually, my system prompt identity card has specific \"Reach for\" and \"Refuse\" lists. The \"Refuse\" list includes \"further analysis, more data would help, arguably, as far as I know, in our experience...\" etc. It does NOT include a ban on em-dashes or tables. But the broader voice-guide-linter (coilyco-voice-guide-linter) says no em-dashes, no prose tables. To be safe and consistent with Kai's house style, I'll avoid em-dashes and use a real table is fine? \"no prose tables\" — tables are fine, \"prose tables\" (tables rendered as text in prose) maybe. A markdown table for price bands is appropriate and not \"prose table.\" Actually the rule \"no prose tables\" likely means don't format tabular data as prose. A markdown table is the clean way. I'll use a markdown table for price bands.\n\nLet me avoid em-dashes entirely (use periods or colons). Avoid italics.\n\nLet me write.\n\nPrice bands summary:\n| Project | One-time cut | Support retainer |\n| umbra | $3,000–$7,500 | $750–$3,000/mo |\n| acompose | $2,250–$6,000 | $750–$2,250/mo |\n\nAll at the confirmed $150/hr rule rate. The one-time hour ranges come from Jev (20–50 hrs umbra, 15–40 hrs acompose). The support hour ranges (5–20 hrs/mo, 5–15 hrs/mo) are my scoping, clearly labeled.\n\nActually, let me reconsider support band numbers to be clean:\n- umbra support: 5–20 hrs/mo → $750–$3,000/mo\n- acompose support: 5–15 hrs/mo → $750–$2,250/mo\n\nI'll label the support hour ranges as my estimate (not Jev).\n\nLet me write the final response now.\n\nFinal answer structure:\n\n**The call** (first): Price umbra's one-time cut at $3k–$7.5k and acompose's at $2k–$6k, both on the confirmed $150/hr rate; but no, don't count on either selling as-is, because there is no buyer channel and the decision model puts a paying buyer at 38% and 37%.\n\nThen the guardrail sequence.\n\nLet me make sure to name the gate. The \"gate\" here is: \"the offer stays a hypothesis until there's an inbound buyer ask or a buyer-facing artifact.\"\n\nLet me write it now, being careful about voice and length (concise, not bloated).",
