          "type": "text",
          "text": "## The call\n\nPrice bands are arithmetic off the one confirmed number we have, the $150/hr rule rate. One-time cuts: **umbra $3k–$7.5k, acompose $2k–$6k**. But no, I would not count on either selling as it stands. The decision model puts a paying buyer at 38% (umbra) and 37% (acompose), and the one fact that decides it is this: there is no technical-buyer channel, only practitioner surfaces, and the guardrail layer umbra overlaps is already commoditized. The gate is an inbound buyer ask in their own terms; until that shows up, both are hypotheses.\n\n## Price bands\n\nEvery number here is the confirmed $150/hr rule rate multiplied by hours. I did not invent a new rate or a premium tier, because the compensation rule forbids it.\n\n| Project | One-time cut | Long-term support |\n|---|---|---|\n| umbra | $3,000–$7,500 | $750–$3,000/mo |\n| acompose | $2,250–$6,000 | $750–$2,250/mo |\n\n- **umbra one-time (20–50 hrs):** Jev scored the realistic cut at \"20 to 50 hours\" (0.73, confidence 0.72). It is integration, not greenfield: scope the buyer's verbs, author guardfiles, wire the egress proxy and audit log, deploy into their runtime.\n- **acompose one-time (15–40 hrs):** Jev scored it \"15 to 40 hours\" (0.74, confidence 0.73). It is roster authoring plus harness wiring.\n- **Support bands are my scoping, not a verdict.** Retainer arithmetic at $150/hr: 5–20 hrs/mo for umbra, 5–15 hrs/mo for acompose. These hour counts are my estimate and are not confirmed anywhere.\n\nModel: jev-1.13.0 (I asked for jev-latest; that is what it resolved to).\n\n## Would anyone actually buy one\n\n**Case against, first.** The strongest reason the answer is \"no\" is not the work. It is that Kai reaches practitioners, not buyers. The live formats (code freeze, streams, talks) speak to workers. A technical buyer wants a problem named in their own terms with the cost of leaving it unfixed attached, and that artifact does not exist. On top of that, the outside frame says the commodity is already here: Kong AI Gateway 3.13 shipped default-deny MCP tool ACLs, and Red Hat, Cerbos, ToolHive, and Permit.io are all in the same space as umbra's layer. A buyer with budget has cheaper, self-serve alternatives (AgentGuard SDK is $39–$79/mo). \"Contracting stays a hypothesis unless evidence establishes it\" is the portfolio's own rule, and there is no customer, no inbound ask, no revenue to point at. I checked the outside frame: three searches, roughly eighteen results, and the direction is uniform.\n\n**What would reverse it.** One buyer asking in their own words (\"how do I bound what my agent can touch on my infra,\" \"can this render my roster on my harness\"). That is the missing instrument, and the doctrine names it: a written buyer-facing artifact carrying a consequence, not another demonstration. Or a second operated subject that raises the portfolio's n-of-one ceiling.\n\n**The verdict, sourced outside this seat.** Jev: 38% toward \"a buyer would pay\" for umbra, 37% for acompose. Both lean no, and the two are statistically indistinguishable, so the honest read is \"neither clears the bar today, and there is no evidence yet that either will.\"\n\n**Which one, if forced.** Qualitatively umbra is the one that clears first: its buyer problem (an agent with a shell) is more urgent, and it ships no model so NDAA Section 1532 leaves it sayable in defense-adjacent rooms. acompose is thinner and narrower. That is a read, not a Jev number.\n\n## What I am not doing\n\nI am not pricing these as a real offering and I am not claiming a customer base exists. Those stay hypotheses until an inbound buyer or a buyer-facing artifact lands. The wording for that artifact is not mine to write; it belongs to the advocate seat, and this is the factual record for it to work from."
